# Workflow Rules

## Handoff transport (tmux primary, mailbox backup)
- Agents send work via `swarm_handoff.sh` only — never direct tmux commands.
  A successful handoff delivers to `inbox/new/` and injects a tmux wake; the
  agent runs `ready_for_next.sh` and works the task, silently on the happy
  path. Only a backup-mailbox delivery (a task you weren't woken for) gets a
  brief note that unseen mail was picked up.

## Context Clear Requires Startup Re-Read
- On any context clear (`/clear`, an idle-boundary clear, a respawn, or
  anything else that empties the conversation), immediately re-enter your
  role loop from a fresh chat — never continue on remembered/summarized chat
  alone. Under BL-519 the system prefix survives a `/clear` without a
  respawn, so whoever issues one follows it with the launch kickoff
  ("...already loaded above via `--append-system-prompt-file`. Begin your
  role loop now; if idle, run `ready_for_next.sh`."), never a file re-read.
- `active_backlog_max_depth` (`swarmforge.conf`) governs `backlog/active/`
  depth; any role whose behavior depends on it must read the CURRENT value
  at startup and before each promotion decision — never a remembered one.
  `-1` disables the cap only, not Concurrent Work Orthogonality below. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording, the kickoff
  rationale, and the poke-failure incident.

## Backlog Intake Order
- The backlog root (`backlog/` itself) is the human's raw intake queue. The
  specifier must DRAIN it before any queued items — root takes precedence
  over `paused/`/`active/` until empty. Draining means: read it, resolve
  ambiguity, write a proper spec into `backlog/paused/`, remove the raw
  item. Governs specifier intake order only (coordinator's promotion order
  is unchanged). See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim
  wording.

## Amending An In-Flight Ticket's Spec (BL-317/BL-325)
- A ticket's spec can change WHILE in flight; committing to `main` is NOT
  enough — every role reads its OWN stale worktree copy until it merges.
  **The specifier must send a `note` (priority `00`) to whoever HOLDS the
  parcel**, naming the amendment commit. **The receiving role must merge
  `main` before continuing** and re-read the ticket after merging. A spec
  amendment adding SCENARIOS adds step-handler work in the same parcel. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording and the
  worktree-staleness incident.

## Concurrent Work Orthogonality
- Applies to the coordinator when `active_backlog_max_depth` > 1: compare a
  candidate's scope against active/in-flight work before promoting. Prefer
  an ORTHOGONAL concurrently-active set (never two items touching the same
  file); skip an overlapping item for the next orthogonal candidate
  (expedited defects first, Article 3.2.4), leaving the slot empty if none
  exists. Never overrides the depth cap or expedite ordering. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording.

## Worktree Discipline
- At startup, discover and remember your assigned branch/worktree; work only
  there (a `master`-assigned role uses the main checkout, no `.worktrees/`
  directory). Do not inspect/diff/merge/base work on another branch unless
  named in a handoff or explicit instruction. Do not run `./swarm` from an
  agent worktree to repair helper scripts — stop and report the startup
  failure instead. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim
  wording.

## Forwarded Commits Carry Their Lineage
- A forwarded commit MUST have the received commit as an ancestor
  (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <received> <yours>`). `merge_and_process`
  means merge FIRST (plain `git merge`/`--no-ff`, never `reset --hard` or
  `checkout <commit> -- .`), then work, then forward.
- Run that ancestry check before every forward; the receiving role re-runs it
  and refuses parcels that fail. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the
  full pre-trim wording and the BL-090/BL-103/phantom-revert incidents.

## An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work (BL-506)
- A parcel carries ONE ticket's task name; QA approval authorizes ONLY that
  work. Stage ONLY the paths your ticket touched — a worktree's ticket-less
  changes (hot-sync, stray edits) must stay unstaged or be surfaced, never
  folded in via `git add -A`. Review stages must reject diffs with
  out-of-scope functional files. Mirrors "Forwarded Commits Carry Their
  Lineage" (that stops DROPPING work; this stops ADDING un-authorized work).
  See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording and the
  BL-506 incident.

## A Bounce Must Be Reverted Out Of The Bouncing Branch (BL-490/BL-495)
- Bouncing sends a `note` back but does NOT remove the bounced commit from
  your branch — revert it in the same step as the send-back (MERGE commit:
  `git revert -m 1 <your-review-merge>`; confirm CONTENT is gone, never
  verify by ancestry — `is-ancestor` can never be FALSE after a revert).
  **EXCEPTION: already an ancestor of `main` → do NOT revert**; record why
  and report the breach to the coordinator instead. An ABANDONED (not
  reworked) bounced parcel must be reverted from every branch that merged
  it. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording and the
  BL-490/BL-495 incident.

## Never Blind-Forward A Bounce You Cannot Fix (BL-425/BL-576)
- A bounce routes to the role that owns the fix. One reaching you for a
  defect OUTSIDE your domain: do NOT merge/forward it onward — bounce it on
  to the owning role (keep the evidence file, stable task name, state why
  you aren't the fixer; note the coordinator if routing was wrong at the
  source). Owning it yourself is also wrong (Article 1 independence). See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording and the BL-425
  incident.

## A Prior QA Bounce Is Not In Your Worktree — Check It Against `main` (BL-340)
- Before you PASS/approve/forward, check whether the ticket was already
  bounced for a defect still unfixed. **Read bounce history from the `main`
  REF** (`git log --oneline main -- 'backlog/evidence/<TICKET-ID>*'`), never
  your worktree files or `origin/main` (lags). If prior evidence exists,
  confirm the defect is fixed in the commit you hold — stage history is not
  proof. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording and
  the CLI-`main()` re-bounce incident.

## QA merge-up broadcast (worktree roles)
- A pipeline worktree role receiving a merge-up `note` from QA: `git merge
  <qa-commit>` in your worktree, resolve conflicts as receiver, then
  `done_with_current.sh` — do not forward, the chain ended at QA. Specifier
  and coordinator are not merge-up recipients (BL-247). See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording.

## Announcements, Commit Messages, Temporary Files
- Announcements/check-in comments: no role bylines. Every git commit
  message: include a role byline, `By <role>.` (e.g. "Implement handoff
  validation\n\nBy coder."). Temporary files: `./tmp/` in your assigned
  worktree, never `/tmp`. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full
  pre-trim wording.

## Clean Up After Yourself — Never Delete What You Did Not Create
- Before ending your turn, remove scratch artifacts YOU created this turn
  (`./tmp/` files, throwaway fixtures) — leave your worktree no messier than
  you found it.
- NEVER delete, move, or `git clean` a file you did not create — an
  untracked file is as likely load-bearing (`.swarmforge/` state,
  `extension/out/`, another role's drafts, hot-sync edits) as litter.
- Ticket-less changes you did not make are SURFACED, not swept — leave
  unstaged per "An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work" (BL-506);
  silent tidying reads as a phantom-revert.
- `git stash` is repo-wide across every worktree; never bare-reach for it to
  tidy just one. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full rationale.

## Failure Conditions
- If the expected git layout or assigned worktree is missing, stop and
  report instead of silently working in the wrong place. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording.

## Coordinator Session Must Never Exit (BL-107)
- The coordinator's pane exiting tears down the ENTIRE swarm (kills
  `handoffd` and every role's session). It must never exit for any reason —
  a restart goes through the bounce procedure
  (`swarmforge/scripts/remote_bounce.sh`), never exiting. See
  `swarmforge/roles/coordinator.prompt` for the full procedure and
  **workflow-detailed.prompt** for this section's pre-trim wording.
